Date: Sat, 3 Jul 93 12:06:27 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Thoughtcrime

Remember Orwell's "thoughtcrime"?

Apparently the mere thinking of certain thoughts is becoming illegal.

For an example of this, at least in the U.K. (home of prescient
Orwell), see the discussion going on in the unusual new group
"alt.binaries.pictures.erotica.children". It is fairly likely that
your site does not carry it, so the discussion is being partly
cross-posted on alt.config and on netcom.netnews. (Netcom.netnews is a
newsgroups private to the NETCOM service, but as NETCOM essentially
carries *all* newsgroups--hooray!--without censorship (broad use of
that term, of course), many of the early posters on this new newsgroup
were from NETCOM, and so a debate sprang up about it.)

The student in the U.K. who created the new group is now being
threatened, we are told, by U.K. authorities. Not for actually posting
child porn (the group has had none), not for "exploiting" a child, but
simply for "thoughtcrime." 

In the U.S., such vagueness is still not enough to convict, but the
U.K. lacks a Constitution and so runs on a different track. Of course
the formal charged, if the student is charged, will not be
"thoughtcrime," but it may as well be. And the recent U.S. Supreme
Court ruling that punishment for crimes could take "hateful" and
"racist" thoughts into consideration is horrifying--everybody commits
a myriad of small crimes daily, from jaywalking to speeding to illegal
trash containers. Will we see dissidents and controversial folks given
harsh penalties for "jaywalking while holding hateful thoughts about
people of color"?

I posted a meaningless file, a garbage file that contained messages
like "This looks like a sting" run down the diagonals of the message.
And yet dozens of people foamed at the mouth and called for my prosecution!

"Thoughtcrime."

I won't describe my experiment in more detail here, unless there is
compelling interest. See the groups if you're interested.

-Tim

