Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech #005

In <9307012118.AA03540@wookumz.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)  wrote:
> 
> James A. Donald <jamesdon@infoserv.com> writes more:
> > 
> > Hard deterministic machines in a cold vacuum
> > cannot work for components smaller than a few micrometers, at least
> > they cannot work with components that anyone has designed so far.
> 
> I don't know whether this means Drexler's designs can't work for
> theoretical reasons, or no working nanomachines have been
> *demonstrated* so far...I guess the former.

Cannot work for theoretical reasons.

To avoid freezing up, the machine must have substantial
internal degrees of freedom that are in random motion.

Number of uncontrolled degrees of freedom times kT must be
greater than the binding energy of the van der Waals forces
that are tending to freeze up the bearings.

Hence no determinism.

>
> The parts of nanomachines have clearances around them.
> 
> They never slide against each other.  They pivot and
> roll, or they glide through holes with clearance.

Not so.  You have to make or break contact in order to have
cams and the like, for example his proposed mechanical
computers.  Your wheels need axles, your bearings need
cages.  Furthermore a wheel will still have static rolling
resistance.  If a wheel is subject to a large force,
pushing it against the surface on which it rolls, the
static friction will be considerable.  If the wheel is
bumpy, because it is made out of discrete individual atoms,
the static friction will be considerable.

In a well designed Drexler style nanomachine, the static
resistance would be much smaller than for protein
molecules, so it would not be necessary to take such
extreme measures against static resistance as are taken in
biological systems.  As a result such machines would be
more comprehensible than biological systems.

But Drexler's assumption that the static resistance would
be negligible, so that a nanomachine can operate in a fully
deterministic manner, does not appear likely.  Scaling laws
lead to the expectation that static friction, not
insignificant in sewing machines, would be a vastly more
serious problem in nano scale machines.

Nanomachines are possible, but are not going to be as
simple as Drexler expects.

> Drexler machines look nothing like micro machines, but in a 
> strange way they look like macro machines again, since once
> again you know the exact shapes and behaviors of the parts
> (atoms), which are once again smooth.

Atoms are not "smooth".  Van der Waals forces are not a
simple uniform attraction of every atom for every other
atom in the vicinity, as Drexler imagines.  They are
complex, irregular and sticky.  A shift of one atom is
likely to alter the forces between several other atoms in
the vicinity.

A wheel of atoms is bumpy  A fullerene sphere
resting on an atomically flat graphite surface requires
measurable force to make it roll, even though it spins in
place by merely thermal forces.

When you measure fullerene spheres on an atomically flat
graphite surface using an atomic force microscope, you do
not have the problems that you would have if you were
trying to measure marbles on glass using a policeman's
truncheon.  They stay put, though they spin in place.  The
microscopic equivalent of what macroscopically we call
static friction.

I doubt that Drexler's wheels would be as perfect as a
fullerene sphere on a graphite surface.

The van der Waals force is not a simple atom to atom
force, it is a system of atoms to system of atoms force. 
Because it is too complex to calculate, people approximate
by a simple r^-6 attraction.  But this approximation is
drastically false.

