>       field than as sliding subject to friction.  

This would be perfectly true if the force field could be
made uniform in the direction of motion.  But the "force
field" is bumpy.  The nanomachine will tend to settle into
the bumps and stay put unless shoved out of the bumps.  The
bumps are very substantial.

This is the nano scale equivalent of what macroscopically
we call static friction.

>       Static friction
>       (in the conventional sense) does not exist in well-designed
>       eutactic bearings: its closest analogues -- energy barriers
>       and force gradients  ....

The "analog" of static friction, will have results exactly
same as static friction, unless the entropy of the random
motion of many internal degrees of freedom of the
nanomachine is greater than the energy barrier.  Drexler
needs to evaluate roughly how large this "analog" is going
to be.  He makes not attempt to do so, perhaps because
there is every reason to expect it to be very large for
large nanomachines. containing many tightly linked parts.

>       its closest analogues -- energy barriers
>       and force gradients  ...-- can be lower for two coupled objects
>       sliding over a surface than for either object considered
>       separately (Section 10.8) even though their contact forces
>       are additive. 

Consider two randomly oriented graphite planes, as in
highly oriented pyrolytic graphite.  By Drexler's arguments
(10.8) there should be systematic cancellation in the
energy barrier against one layer sliding over the other
because the two planes are randomly oriented, so the layers
should easily slide.  Certainly HOPG is not strong against
shear forces, some systematic cancellation does occur, but
unless surface fluids become entrained between the layers,
it is not that weak either.  Because of the collective
nature of van der Waals forces, the systematic cancellation
is much less than one would expect from naive arguments
that treat van der Waals forces as pairwise independent, as
Drexler does.

The drastic effect of a few contaminants entrained between
layers of HOPG shows that lubrication makes an enormous
difference, reducing static friction be a factor of
hundreds or thousands.

This shows that lubrication has the same effect on the
atomic scale, as it does on the macroscopic scale. 
Nanomachines will need lubrication.  Because lubricant
molecules are comparable in size to nanomachine
components, nano machines will need to float in a large
amount of lubricant fluid, as biological systems do.

Molecules analogous to those proposed by Drexler exist.
Their behavior is roughly what you would expect by scaling.
Nanomachines will suffer the same problems as
micromachines do, only more so.


