Subject: PAGANISM: one more resource

A good ongoing source of information on paganism and related
manners is GNOSIS magazine. Subtitled "A Journal of the Western
Inner Traditions," I have always found this to be an interesting
and informative read. Published quarterly, each issue is devoted
to a particular theme. Recent themes are listed below, along with
some sample article titles, follow:
 
 - Gnosticism Revisited (#23, spring '92). Articles include:
   "Gnosticism and Social Action" by Charles Upton
   "Tongues of Fire, Gifts of Noise" by Erik Davis
    (The blurb for this one says: "We live in an information age
     and work in an information economy. But what's information
     theory got to do with your spiritual life?")
 
 - Saints and Scoundrels (#24, summer '92) Articles include:
   "Choose Your Saints Wisely" by Richard Smoley
   "The Hell-Fire Club" by Daniel Willens
   "The Best of the Beast" by Antoinette LaFarge & Robert Allen.
    (No issue on scoudrels would be complete w/o a mention of
     ALeister Crowley)
 
 - Groups and Communities (#25, fall '92)
 
 - Psychedelics and the Path (#26, winter '93). great issue, with
   views both pro and con on the viability of psychedelics as a
   source of enlightenment.
 
 - Sacred Art and Music (# 27, spring '93). Articles on gothic
   cathedrals, esoteric sound and color, and tattoos (!).
 
In addition, each issue contains a couple of regular columns
(including one by Ted Scultz, who edited Whole Earth's Fringes
of Reason catalog), a lively letters section, and a lot of book
reviews of books on a variety of esoteric subjects. The tone is
thoughtful and scholarly but not stuffy, and definitely not
'new-agey' in the trippy/dippy/hippy sense of of the word. No
ads for crystal healing here folks. (Like the Firesign Theater
said: "There's a seeker born every minute.") Visually, this is a
handsome publication. If it sounds like it's up your alley, I
urge you to support this publication, as it is one of those
fringe publications that is a always just breaking even.
Subscriptions are $18/year, and back issues are $6. If you want
to check it out, I have seen it at better bookstores.
 
